do you develop distributed applications  or systems made up of lots of  microservices if so you've surely had to  deal with issues where users complain  about the service being slow  in a distributed system it's often hard  to figure out which dependencies causing  it  this is where distributed tracing comes  in  we covered using open telemetry to  capture and analyze latency from  distributed applications in the previous  episode if you haven't watched it we'll  link to it in the episode notes but in  brief we want to create traces for user  requests and spans for the service to  service requests whose latency we want  to analyze to determine which services  may be contributing the most to the  overall latency experience for our users  open telemetry is an open source library  that allows us to easily instrument our  services and capture traces from them my  name is yuri i'm an sre at google cloud  and today i'm going to show you two ways  to instrument your services with open  telemetry i'll cover easily getting  started with automatic instrumentation  and using manual instrumentation for  maximum control welcome back to  engineering for reliability  [Music]  let's have a look at a simple  application that has a front end that  serves user requests and makes its own  request to a backend to retrieve  additional data we'll use this for both  of our examples today so ideally we want  to capture the latency of the overall  request that's perceived by the user the  latency of the backend request as seen  by the front end and how long the  backend processing takes  the front end of our application is  written in flask and it uses a request  library to make back-end calls  the back-end is also a flashcard when it  receives a request it does some  processing before returning a response  to use automatic instrumentation we can  run both our front end and back end  using the open telemetry instrument  command the command will try to  automatically detect packages used by  our code and apply automatic tracing  instrumentation on them we get  distributed tracing exported to  cloudtrace without having to make any  code changes let's take a look  our front end is running locally  using the open telemetry instrument  command  instructing it to send traces to  cloudtrace  and the backend is running locally as  well using the same command  let's send a request to our application  and confirm the response  in the cloud trace ui  we can find the resulting trace  it's comprised of multiple spans  the root span is the  overall request  and we can see how long it took  it was made to our service running on  localhost on port 8080.  the first child span is the backend  request  it was also made to localhost  but on port 8081  which is where our backend service is  running  so this is how you can use open  telemetry automatic instrumentation to  capture traces across distributed  services  without the need to modify application  code at all  that was really easy but there is a  caveat we couldn't create a custom span  to time back-end processing without  adding instrumentation in the code so  let's have a look at how you can  manually instrument your service to  explicitly control how spans are created  we'll link to the full code sample in  the episode notes but let's start by  reviewing our front end code  [Music]  first we need to import the necessary  open telemetry libraries that will let  us manage spans and context propagation  between our services we'll use the  request contacts to send information  about our trace to the back end so that  it can append its own spans to it  next we configure our exporter to send  traces to cloudtrace  finally we're ready to make our backend  request we create our root span that  will serve as the parent for the  subsequent spans  next we create a span specifically to  time how long the backend request takes  lastly we make a request with http  headers that will carry the span context  to our back end  in our back end service we first extract  the span context from the incoming  request headers  we then create a dedicated span using  that context to time how long our  backend processing task takes now let's  run our services and inspect our trace  in cloud trace  in this example we also have our front  end running locally but this time we're  using the basic python command  our backend is also running locally  using the same command  we can confirm that our application is  responding by sending a request and  confirming the response  now we can go to cloudtrace  and find the resulting trace in the  trace list  the trace has our root span  the back end request span  and now a dedicated span for back-end  processing  for each of these we can see exactly how  long they took  which allows us to figure out exactly  where the time is spent in serving the  user request  so this is how you can use manual  instrumentation with open telemetry  to send request context over the wire  and use it to append child spans from  downstream services  thanks so much for watching today we'll  learn how to use both automatic and  manual instrumentation with open  telemetry for distributed tracing of our  python services and understand latency  in distributed application  i hope this helps you to keep your  services reliable and your users happy  don't forget to like and subscribe to  never miss out on more engineering for  reliability with google cloud see you  soon  [Music]
 

